  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 117 AM MDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nIt has been an active few months, with everything from all time \nrecord heat to a monsoon that seemed like it never wanted to end (it \nlooks like it has finally ended, thankfully), with seemingly \nsomething happening each day. You know it wouldn't be one my \ndiscussions without an old movie reference referring to the weather \npattern. And today, we turn to an 80s movie starring the late, great \nRodney Dangerfield with an assist from Joe Pesci, the movie is Easy \nMoney. While the weather will likely not be completely quiet, it \ngeneral pattern will be, at least compared to many of the previous \nweeks, Easy Money for meteorologists.\n\nDrier air is currently pressing southward into the area from the \nnorthwest. This should bring a pleasant, early autumn day to most of \nthe area. The exception may be Sweetwater County, especially south \nof Interstate 80. Just enough moisture may remain there and they may \nbe on the edge of the moisture from the remnants of Hurricane Polo \nfor there to be a few showers, especially in the morning. I also \ncan't rule out an isolated shower or thunderstorm here in the \nafternoon, but with the chance less than 1 out of 10, we left the \nforecast dry for now. Most locations should be comfortably warm, \nwith highs generally 5 to 10 degrees above normal. Wednesday also \nlooks like a nice autumn day as just about all guidance keeps the \nmoisture from Polo to the south across Colorado.\n\nThings could get a little more active on Thursday as a cold front \ndrops in from the north. The front is of continental origin and \ndoesn't have a ton of moisture to work with though. One thing it \nwill have though is a jet streak moving along it as it drops \nsouthward. This may bring enough lift to produce some showers \nThursday or Thursday night. Most guidance shows this largely north \nof Wyoming, but if the front can drop a bit further south, it could \nimpact portions of Big Horn and Johnson Counties with some showers. \nPOPs are low for now though, generally less than 1 out of 3. Wind \nmay also need to be increased a bit with the cold advection pattern, \nbut high wind is not expected. The main impact will be to knock \ntemperatures down somewhat, especially in a place like Buffalo which \ncould be 10 to 15 degrees cooler than on Wednesday. Most locations \nEast of the Divide will generally be 5 to 10 degrees cooler on \nThursday and possibly Friday as well. \n\nAnother front may get close to Wyoming Friday night or Saturday, but \nthis one is even drier and would only produce very isolated \nshowers, again mainly across northern Wyoming. Following that, a \nvast majority of guidance then rebuilds a strong ridge across the \nwestern Unites States, bringing a prolonged period of above normal \ntemperatures with mainly dry conditions. Some guidance is showing\na shortwave possibly producing a few showers on Monday, but this\nis highly uncertain this far out. So, other than a couple of \nweak systems, the weather looks relatively uneventful across the\narea. And, there are no signs of a big cooldown with temperatures\ngenerally remaining near to above normal over the next several \ndays.",
